text: "<think>\nThe user wants a clear, concise explanation of why the sky is blue during the day and red at sunset, in exactly three short paragraphs. Let me structure this well:\n\n1. First paragraph: Explain Rayleigh scattering and why the sky is blue during the day.\n2. Second paragraph: Explain what changes at sunset — the longer path through the atmosphere.\n3. Third paragraph: Explain why that longer path produces red/orange colors.\n\nLet me keep each paragraph short and focused.\n</think>\n\nDuring the day, sunlight enters the atmosphere and encounters gas molecules (mainly nitrogen and oxygen). These molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of light—blue and violet—much more strongly than longer wavelengths like red and orange. This phenomenon, called Rayleigh scattering, sends blue light in all directions, which is why the sky looks blue when you look up. (Our eyes are also less sensitive to violet, so blue dominates.)\n\nAt sunset, the Sun is near the horizon, so its light must travel through a much thicker slice of the atmosphere to reach your eyes. That long path scatters away most of the blue and green light before it ever gets to you, stripping those colors out of the beam.\n\nWhat remains is the longer-wavelength light—reds"
